# Files whose SHA-256 is pinned in the pre-registration chain must have
# identical bytes on every platform.
tools/accuracy_registry.py -text

# PREREGISTRATION.md is hashed by ops/accuracy-registry.sh and compared against
# the prereg-document record in the chain. A CRLF checkout on Windows changes
# those bytes, the digests stop matching, and the publishing path refuses with
# "UNREGISTERED PROTOCOL DOCUMENT" — a failure that reads like tampering and is
# really just a line ending.
PREREGISTRATION.md -text

# Shell scripts run inside Linux containers. A CRLF checkout makes the shebang
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, which the kernel reports as "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory" — a confusing way to learn about line endings from an image that
# built cleanly.
*.sh -text

# Generated partials are compared byte-for-byte against a fresh render to prove
# no document drifted from the single source. A CRLF checkout on Windows makes
# every line differ from the LF the generators write, so the comparison reports
# "stale" for a file nobody touched — a false failure, which is how a real check
# gets switched off. tools/docs_gate.py normalises newlines defensively; this
# keeps the bytes right in the first place, for every tool that reads them.
partials/* -text

# The committed answer to the questions CI cannot ask the 4 GB database
# (grader status, PIT universe rows, delisting rate). Same reasoning.
ops/data-integrity.json -text
